   "text": "This SMILES is my reading of the drawing: a bleomycin-type glycopeptide core carrying a five-residue peptoid chain and two Hoechst-type groups. I wrote it region by region and did not run it through a parser.\n\n**Bleomycin core (bottom and right of the drawing)**\n- The terminal group is H2N\u2013CH(C(=O)NH2)\u2013CH2\u2013NH\u2013, attached to the CH of the \u03b2-aminoalanine amide.\n- That CH carries a CH2C(=O)NH2 side chain and sits on C2 of the pyrimidine. The pyrimidine has a 6-NH2, a 5-methyl and a 4-carboxamide.\n- The carboxamide nitrogen joins the carbon that bears the imidazol-4-yl group and the glycosyl ether oxygen.\n- Next come the methyl-hydroxy-valeryl unit and the threonine unit.\n- These lead into the CH2CH2 link to the bithiazole.\n- The disaccharide is a gulose-type ring whose C2 oxygen links to a carbamoyl-bearing second sugar. Both sugars have unspecified stereochemistry because no wedges are drawn on them.\n\n**Linker and peptoid (middle of the drawing)**\n- The bithiazole carboxamide connects through a CH2CH2CH2\u2013NH\u2013(CH2)4\u2013NH\u2013 chain to the peptoid.\n- The peptoid has five N-substituted glycine residues. Reading from the acetyl end, these are N(Ac)(CH2-triazole), three N-propyl residues, and a final N(CH2-triazole) residue.\n- The last residue links back through CH2C(=O)NH to the butyl chain.\n\n**Hoechst arms (top)**\n- Each 1,4-disubstituted triazole carries N\u2013CH2CH2CH2\u2013NH\u2013C(=O)CH2CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013C6H4.\n- The aryl group is attached to a benzimidazole, which bears a 4-(4-methylpiperazin-1-yl)phenyl group.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The wedged hydroxyl on the methyl-hydroxy-valeryl carbon is encoded as drawn.\n- The glycosylated carbon and the terminal amino acid carbon are encoded as well. For the terminal carbon I assumed the L configuration.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N(Cc1cn(CCCNC(=O)CCCOc2ccc(cc2)-c2nc3cc(ccc3[nH]2)-c2ccc(cc2)N2CCN(C)CC2)nn1)CC(=O)N(CCC)CC(=O)N(CCC)CC(=O)N(CCC)CC(=O)N(Cc1cn(CCCNC(=O)